1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,699
Your build is green.

2
00:00:01,699 --> 00:00:02,597
The tests pass.

3
00:00:02,597 --> 00:00:05,294
The jar is sitting in a folder called target.

4
00:00:05,294 --> 00:00:05,833
Now what?

5
00:00:06,383 --> 00:00:07,296
On one laptop,

6
00:00:07,296 --> 00:00:08,209
in one folder,

7
00:00:08,209 --> 00:00:09,252
it helps nobody.

8
00:00:09,252 --> 00:00:11,404
The next developer cannot use it.

9
00:00:11,404 --> 00:00:13,360
The pipeline cannot deploy it.

10
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:14,534
And in six months,

11
00:00:14,534 --> 00:00:17,925
nobody can say which build is running in production.

12
00:00:18,475 --> 00:00:20,436
A build result needs a home,

13
00:00:20,436 --> 00:00:21,206
with rules.

14
00:00:21,206 --> 00:00:21,626
Today,

15
00:00:21,626 --> 00:00:23,447
Cloud Cart's core library,

16
00:00:23,447 --> 00:00:24,147
cart-core,

17
00:00:24,147 --> 00:00:25,408
gets exactly that.

18
00:00:25,958 --> 00:00:28,067
We will create two repositories,

19
00:00:28,067 --> 00:00:31,231
a deployer that can do one job and nothing else,

20
00:00:31,231 --> 00:00:34,197
and ship a snapshot and a release with Maven.

21
00:00:34,197 --> 00:00:34,856
All of it,

22
00:00:34,856 --> 00:00:35,186
live,

23
00:00:35,186 --> 00:00:36,241
on a real Nexus.

24
00:00:36,791 --> 00:00:37,610
And at the end,

25
00:00:37,610 --> 00:00:39,523
you will fetch your own files back,

26
00:00:39,523 --> 00:00:41,655
the way a teammate or a pipeline would.

27
00:00:43,205 --> 00:00:44,634
Here is what to expect.

28
00:00:45,184 --> 00:00:50,816
One: two ideas, in pictures. A snapshot is work in progress. A release is a promise.

29
00:00:51,366 --> 00:00:52,582
Two: a live setup.

30
00:00:52,582 --> 00:00:53,731
Two repositories,

31
00:00:53,731 --> 00:00:54,339
one role,

32
00:00:54,339 --> 00:00:54,947
one user,

33
00:00:54,947 --> 00:00:59,473
and a Maven configuration that keeps the password out of your code.

34
00:01:00,023 --> 00:01:01,089
Three: the proof.

35
00:01:01,089 --> 00:01:02,656
We ship a snapshot twice,

36
00:01:02,656 --> 00:01:03,909
then a release once.

37
00:01:03,909 --> 00:01:05,915
We try to overwrite the release,

38
00:01:05,915 --> 00:01:07,357
and watch Nexus say no.

39
00:01:07,357 --> 00:01:09,175
Then we fetch our files back.

40
00:01:09,725 --> 00:01:15,500
This lesson covers three objectives of the Nexus domain: snapshot and release repositories,

41
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:17,150
publishing and retrieving,

42
00:01:17,150 --> 00:01:19,752
and Maven settings with safe credentials.

43
00:01:21,302 --> 00:01:23,495
First idea: two kinds of version.

44
00:01:23,495 --> 00:01:25,556
A snapshot is work in progress.

45
00:01:25,556 --> 00:01:28,081
Its version ends in the word SNAPSHOT,

46
00:01:28,081 --> 00:01:29,942
and it may change every day.

47
00:01:30,492 --> 00:01:32,511
Each time you deploy a snapshot,

48
00:01:32,511 --> 00:01:34,404
Nexus keeps it as a new build,

49
00:01:34,404 --> 00:01:36,801
with a date and time in the file name.

50
00:01:36,801 --> 00:01:40,839
A teammate who asks for the snapshot always gets the newest one.

51
00:01:41,389 --> 00:01:42,939
A release is a promise.

52
00:01:42,939 --> 00:01:44,962
Version one point oh point oh,

53
00:01:44,962 --> 00:01:45,974
once published,

54
00:01:45,974 --> 00:01:46,918
never changes.

55
00:01:46,918 --> 00:01:47,524
Tomorrow,

56
00:01:47,524 --> 00:01:48,199
next year,

57
00:01:48,199 --> 00:01:49,210
on any machine,

58
00:01:49,210 --> 00:01:50,626
it is the same bytes.

59
00:01:51,176 --> 00:01:54,138
That is why they are kept in two repositories,

60
00:01:54,138 --> 00:01:55,747
with two different rules.

61
00:01:55,747 --> 00:01:57,486
In the snapshot repository,

62
00:01:57,486 --> 00:01:59,224
deploying again is allowed.

63
00:01:59,224 --> 00:02:00,898
In the release repository,

64
00:02:00,898 --> 00:02:01,928
it is forbidden.

65
00:02:02,478 --> 00:02:06,254
So the rule is simple: snapshot can change, release never does.

66
00:02:07,804 --> 00:02:09,744
Second idea: who may publish.

67
00:02:09,744 --> 00:02:12,687
A developer's own account is the wrong tool.

68
00:02:12,687 --> 00:02:14,760
We create a deployer: one role,

69
00:02:14,760 --> 00:02:15,362
one user,

70
00:02:15,362 --> 00:02:17,302
with only what the job needs.

71
00:02:17,302 --> 00:02:19,308
Write to our two repositories.

72
00:02:19,308 --> 00:02:20,780
Read the public group.

73
00:02:20,780 --> 00:02:21,649
Nothing else.

74
00:02:22,199 --> 00:02:23,962
If that password ever leaks,

75
00:02:23,962 --> 00:02:25,913
the damage is two repositories.

76
00:02:25,913 --> 00:02:26,984
Not the settings.

77
00:02:26,984 --> 00:02:28,243
Not the other users.

78
00:02:28,243 --> 00:02:29,879
Not the other teams' code.

79
00:02:30,429 --> 00:02:33,297
Third idea: how Maven finds the credentials.

80
00:02:33,297 --> 00:02:35,253
The pom says where to publish,

81
00:02:35,253 --> 00:02:37,404
and gives each repository an I D.

82
00:02:37,404 --> 00:02:39,685
The settings file says who you are,

83
00:02:39,685 --> 00:02:40,793
for the same I D.

84
00:02:40,793 --> 00:02:42,031
The two must match,

85
00:02:42,031 --> 00:02:43,205
letter for letter.

86
00:02:43,205 --> 00:02:45,747
And the password never goes in the pom.

87
00:02:46,297 --> 00:02:50,030
So: the deployer has one job. And the I Ds must match.

88
00:02:51,580 --> 00:02:55,121
We continue from the end of the last lesson: the same Nexus,

89
00:02:55,121 --> 00:02:56,538
with the setup finished,

90
00:02:56,538 --> 00:02:57,895
and signed in as admin.

91
00:02:57,895 --> 00:03:00,433
We start with where the files will be kept.

92
00:03:02,483 --> 00:03:03,128
Settings,

93
00:03:03,128 --> 00:03:04,275
then Repository,

94
00:03:04,275 --> 00:03:05,566
then Repositories.

95
00:03:05,566 --> 00:03:09,221
This is the list of every repository on the server,

96
00:03:09,221 --> 00:03:11,443
the six that Nexus made for us.

97
00:03:11,993 --> 00:03:16,644
At the top right is the button that creates a new one. We click Create repository.

98
00:03:18,794 --> 00:03:24,149
Nexus asks for the format first. Cloud Cart is built with Maven, so we choose Maven.

99
00:03:26,099 --> 00:03:27,078
Then the type.

100
00:03:27,078 --> 00:03:30,297
Hosted: a repository that holds our own files.

101
00:03:30,297 --> 00:03:32,605
Proxy and group we met last time.

102
00:03:33,955 --> 00:03:37,945
Now the settings. The name says what it holds: cloudcart-snapshots.

103
00:03:38,495 --> 00:03:41,857
The version policy decides which versions it accepts.

104
00:03:41,857 --> 00:03:44,395
This repository is for work in progress,

105
00:03:44,395 --> 00:03:45,791
so we choose Snapshot.

106
00:03:49,641 --> 00:03:53,671
The deployment policy decides whether a file may be replaced.

107
00:03:53,671 --> 00:03:55,720
A snapshot is replaced all day,

108
00:03:55,720 --> 00:03:57,107
so we allow redeploy.

109
00:04:00,057 --> 00:04:03,705
We click Create repository. The list now has our first one.

110
00:04:04,255 --> 00:04:09,077
Second repository, the same steps. Create repository, Maven, Hosted.

111
00:04:12,027 --> 00:04:15,739
The name: cloudcart-releases. The version policy: Release.

112
00:04:16,289 --> 00:04:19,764
And here is the most important setting of the lesson.

113
00:04:19,764 --> 00:04:22,125
Deployment policy: disable redeploy.

114
00:04:22,125 --> 00:04:24,288
Once one point oh point oh is in,

115
00:04:24,288 --> 00:04:25,862
nobody can overwrite it.

116
00:04:25,862 --> 00:04:28,747
This is what turns a release into a promise.

117
00:04:29,297 --> 00:04:32,796
We create it. Two repositories, ready to receive files.

118
00:04:35,346 --> 00:04:35,671
Next,

119
00:04:35,671 --> 00:04:37,099
who may write to them.

120
00:04:37,099 --> 00:04:37,683
In Nexus,

121
00:04:37,683 --> 00:04:39,890
permissions are called privileges,

122
00:04:39,890 --> 00:04:41,967
and they are grouped into roles.

123
00:04:41,967 --> 00:04:43,525
So we make a role first.

124
00:04:43,525 --> 00:04:44,110
Security,

125
00:04:44,110 --> 00:04:44,824
then Roles,

126
00:04:44,824 --> 00:04:45,927
then Create role.

127
00:04:52,077 --> 00:04:54,353
The I D and the name: cart-deployer.

128
00:04:54,353 --> 00:04:55,491
And a description,

129
00:04:55,491 --> 00:04:58,904
so that in a year somebody knows why this role exists.

130
00:05:00,954 --> 00:05:02,220
Now the privileges.

131
00:05:02,220 --> 00:05:04,020
This button opens the list,

132
00:05:04,020 --> 00:05:04,953
with a filter.

133
00:05:04,953 --> 00:05:06,153
We filter by name,

134
00:05:06,153 --> 00:05:10,752
and tick the privilege that gives every action on cloudcart-releases.

135
00:05:10,752 --> 00:05:13,285
Then the same for cloudcart-snapshots.

136
00:05:15,835 --> 00:05:17,030
Look at the names.

137
00:05:17,030 --> 00:05:19,221
Each privilege says which format,

138
00:05:19,221 --> 00:05:20,349
which repository,

139
00:05:20,349 --> 00:05:21,478
and which action.

140
00:05:21,478 --> 00:05:23,337
The star means every action.

141
00:05:23,337 --> 00:05:26,324
In a real company you would narrow this down.

142
00:05:26,324 --> 00:05:28,847
We do exactly that in the next lesson.

143
00:05:28,847 --> 00:05:29,378
For now,

144
00:05:29,378 --> 00:05:31,237
only these two repositories.

145
00:05:33,287 --> 00:05:35,022
A deployer must also read,

146
00:05:35,022 --> 00:05:40,026
because Maven downloads its plugins and libraries through the public group.

147
00:05:40,026 --> 00:05:41,560
So we add maven-public,

148
00:05:41,560 --> 00:05:42,627
read and browse.

149
00:05:42,627 --> 00:05:44,295
Four privileges in total.

150
00:05:47,345 --> 00:05:49,159
We confirm, and create the role.

151
00:05:55,109 --> 00:05:58,991
Now the user. Security, then Users, then Create local user.

152
00:06:04,541 --> 00:06:06,073
The I D: cart-deployer.

153
00:06:06,073 --> 00:06:06,939
A first name,

154
00:06:06,939 --> 00:06:07,738
a last name,

155
00:06:07,738 --> 00:06:09,137
and an email address.

156
00:06:09,137 --> 00:06:10,535
And a password: long,

157
00:06:10,535 --> 00:06:11,268
and unique.

158
00:06:11,268 --> 00:06:13,866
In a minute you will see where it goes,

159
00:06:13,866 --> 00:06:15,464
and where it never goes.

160
00:06:16,014 --> 00:06:16,633
The roles.

161
00:06:16,633 --> 00:06:18,924
The box on the left lists every role.

162
00:06:18,924 --> 00:06:20,410
We select cart-deployer,

163
00:06:20,410 --> 00:06:21,958
and move it to the right,

164
00:06:21,958 --> 00:06:23,382
into the granted roles.

165
00:06:23,382 --> 00:06:24,249
Only that one.

166
00:06:28,299 --> 00:06:29,509
We create the user,

167
00:06:29,509 --> 00:06:30,655
and open it again.

168
00:06:30,655 --> 00:06:32,820
Further down is the security tree.

169
00:06:32,820 --> 00:06:33,648
We expand it,

170
00:06:33,648 --> 00:06:37,724
and Nexus lists the privileges this user ends up with: our four,

171
00:06:37,724 --> 00:06:38,806
and nothing else.

172
00:06:38,806 --> 00:06:40,335
This is least privilege,

173
00:06:40,335 --> 00:06:41,163
made visible.

174
00:06:43,713 --> 00:06:44,877
Now the Maven side,

175
00:06:44,877 --> 00:06:45,858
in the terminal.

176
00:06:45,858 --> 00:06:47,820
We are in the cart-core project.

177
00:06:47,820 --> 00:06:48,617
It has a pom,

178
00:06:48,617 --> 00:06:49,537
and the source.

179
00:06:50,087 --> 00:06:51,420
Let us read the pom,

180
00:06:51,420 --> 00:06:53,486
only the part about publishing.

181
00:06:53,486 --> 00:06:55,885
The distribution management section.

182
00:06:55,885 --> 00:06:57,018
Two repositories,

183
00:06:57,018 --> 00:06:59,683
and each has an I D: cloudcart-releases,

184
00:06:59,683 --> 00:07:01,283
and cloudcart-snapshots.

185
00:07:01,283 --> 00:07:04,948
Maven picks one by the version: with the word SNAPSHOT,

186
00:07:04,948 --> 00:07:05,681
or without.

187
00:07:06,231 --> 00:07:07,532
Now the settings file,

188
00:07:07,532 --> 00:07:09,719
in the dot M two folder of your home.

189
00:07:09,719 --> 00:07:11,906
It is the file that says who you are.

190
00:07:12,456 --> 00:07:13,345
Three things.

191
00:07:13,345 --> 00:07:13,755
First,

192
00:07:13,755 --> 00:07:15,670
a server entry for each I D.

193
00:07:15,670 --> 00:07:16,969
The same two names,

194
00:07:16,969 --> 00:07:18,199
letter for letter.

195
00:07:18,199 --> 00:07:19,225
If one differs,

196
00:07:19,225 --> 00:07:21,071
Maven sends no credentials,

197
00:07:21,071 --> 00:07:23,464
and Nexus answers with four oh one.

198
00:07:24,014 --> 00:07:24,443
Second,

199
00:07:24,443 --> 00:07:25,731
look at the password.

200
00:07:25,731 --> 00:07:26,590
There is none.

201
00:07:26,590 --> 00:07:29,656
The file reads two variables from the environment,

202
00:07:29,656 --> 00:07:31,557
the user name and the password.

203
00:07:31,557 --> 00:07:32,415
In a pipeline,

204
00:07:32,415 --> 00:07:34,377
they come from its secret store.

205
00:07:34,377 --> 00:07:36,708
Here they are already set in my shell,

206
00:07:36,708 --> 00:07:38,670
and we print only the user name.

207
00:07:39,220 --> 00:07:39,606
Third,

208
00:07:39,606 --> 00:07:40,314
the mirror.

209
00:07:40,314 --> 00:07:42,887
It sends every download to maven-public,

210
00:07:42,887 --> 00:07:44,881
the group from the last lesson.

211
00:07:44,881 --> 00:07:47,647
So even the plugins come through our Nexus.

212
00:07:48,197 --> 00:07:49,288
Now the moment.

213
00:07:49,288 --> 00:07:50,233
M V N deploy.

214
00:07:50,233 --> 00:07:51,324
Maven compiles,

215
00:07:51,324 --> 00:07:52,415
runs the tests,

216
00:07:52,415 --> 00:07:53,506
builds the jar,

217
00:07:53,506 --> 00:07:54,597
and uploads it.

218
00:07:54,597 --> 00:07:57,288
Because the version ends in SNAPSHOT,

219
00:07:57,288 --> 00:07:59,543
it goes to cloudcart-snapshots.

220
00:07:59,543 --> 00:08:00,634
A small filter,

221
00:08:00,634 --> 00:08:01,507
called keep,

222
00:08:01,507 --> 00:08:03,834
prints only the important lines.

223
00:08:04,384 --> 00:08:05,438
The test passed.

224
00:08:05,438 --> 00:08:06,558
Then two uploads,

225
00:08:06,558 --> 00:08:08,667
to cloudcart-snapshots: the jar,

226
00:08:08,667 --> 00:08:09,457
and the pom.

227
00:08:09,457 --> 00:08:10,907
Look at the file name.

228
00:08:10,907 --> 00:08:12,093
After the version,

229
00:08:12,093 --> 00:08:13,147
a date and time,

230
00:08:13,147 --> 00:08:14,399
and a build number.

231
00:08:14,399 --> 00:08:15,387
And at the end,

232
00:08:15,387 --> 00:08:16,309
build success.

233
00:08:18,359 --> 00:08:23,415
In the browser. Browse, then cloudcart-snapshots. Here is the build we just published.

234
00:08:26,965 --> 00:08:28,242
We open the folders,

235
00:08:28,242 --> 00:08:29,519
down to the version.

236
00:08:29,519 --> 00:08:31,497
One point oh point oh snapshot.

237
00:08:31,497 --> 00:08:32,774
The jar and the pom,

238
00:08:32,774 --> 00:08:35,008
with the time stamp in their names.

239
00:08:39,558 --> 00:08:43,398
A snapshot may change. So we deploy again, with the same command.

240
00:08:43,948 --> 00:08:44,870
The same tests,

241
00:08:44,870 --> 00:08:45,853
the same upload.

242
00:08:45,853 --> 00:08:48,311
Look at the new file name: a later time,

243
00:08:48,311 --> 00:08:49,601
and build number two.

244
00:08:50,151 --> 00:08:53,214
What does Maven see when it asks for the snapshot?

245
00:08:53,214 --> 00:08:55,724
Nexus keeps a small file with the answer.

246
00:08:55,724 --> 00:08:58,542
First a short name for the repository address,

247
00:08:58,542 --> 00:09:01,053
then we print only the lines that matter.

248
00:09:02,303 --> 00:09:03,732
The newest time stamp,

249
00:09:03,732 --> 00:09:05,096
and build number two.

250
00:09:05,096 --> 00:09:06,461
Both builds are kept.

251
00:09:06,461 --> 00:09:07,045
The name,

252
00:09:07,045 --> 00:09:09,060
one point oh point oh snapshot,

253
00:09:09,060 --> 00:09:10,879
always points to the latest.

254
00:09:11,429 --> 00:09:12,436
Now the release.

255
00:09:12,436 --> 00:09:15,270
We remove the word SNAPSHOT from the version.

256
00:09:15,270 --> 00:09:18,166
A real pipeline does this with a release tool.

257
00:09:18,166 --> 00:09:18,481
Here,

258
00:09:18,481 --> 00:09:19,237
one command.

259
00:09:20,687 --> 00:09:25,487
The first version line of the pom now says one point oh point oh. No snapshot.

260
00:09:26,037 --> 00:09:31,178
We deploy again. The version has no SNAPSHOT, so Maven picks the other repository.

261
00:09:31,728 --> 00:09:33,882
Uploaded to cloudcart-releases.

262
00:09:33,882 --> 00:09:38,191
The file name has no time stamp at all: one point oh point oh,

263
00:09:38,191 --> 00:09:38,747
exactly.

264
00:09:39,297 --> 00:09:42,753
Now the promise. We deploy the same version a second time.

265
00:09:44,903 --> 00:09:45,810
Build failure.

266
00:09:45,810 --> 00:09:47,950
Look at the status: four oh nine,

267
00:09:47,950 --> 00:09:48,664
a conflict.

268
00:09:48,664 --> 00:09:51,517
The message says the file cannot be updated,

269
00:09:51,517 --> 00:09:53,203
because it already exists,

270
00:09:53,203 --> 00:09:55,018
and redeploy is not allowed.

271
00:09:55,018 --> 00:09:58,520
Version one point oh point oh stays exactly as it was.

272
00:09:58,520 --> 00:10:02,929
This one error is the whole reason for the setting we chose earlier.

273
00:10:03,479 --> 00:10:05,578
Last part: getting our files back.

274
00:10:05,578 --> 00:10:06,258
A teammate,

275
00:10:06,258 --> 00:10:07,122
or a pipeline,

276
00:10:07,122 --> 00:10:09,530
asks for a file by its path: the group,

277
00:10:09,530 --> 00:10:10,085
the name,

278
00:10:10,085 --> 00:10:10,826
the version,

279
00:10:10,826 --> 00:10:11,629
and the file.

280
00:10:14,379 --> 00:10:15,113
Two hundred.

281
00:10:15,113 --> 00:10:16,397
The release is there.

282
00:10:16,397 --> 00:10:19,149
This is the same request a Maven build makes,

283
00:10:19,149 --> 00:10:20,250
behind the scenes,

284
00:10:20,250 --> 00:10:21,717
when it needs cart-core.

285
00:10:22,267 --> 00:10:22,916
Or search.

286
00:10:22,916 --> 00:10:24,667
Nexus has a search service.

287
00:10:24,667 --> 00:10:26,613
We ask for the name cart-core,

288
00:10:26,613 --> 00:10:28,753
and print only the version lines.

289
00:10:29,303 --> 00:10:30,381
Three components.

290
00:10:30,381 --> 00:10:32,094
Two builds of the snapshot,

291
00:10:32,094 --> 00:10:33,681
each with its time stamp,

292
00:10:33,681 --> 00:10:34,696
and the release.

293
00:10:34,696 --> 00:10:35,330
All of it,

294
00:10:35,330 --> 00:10:36,663
found in one command.

295
00:10:37,213 --> 00:10:38,114
One last proof,

296
00:10:38,114 --> 00:10:38,956
about secrets.

297
00:10:38,956 --> 00:10:40,518
We search every file here,

298
00:10:40,518 --> 00:10:42,021
and in the settings file,

299
00:10:42,021 --> 00:10:43,463
for the password itself.

300
00:10:44,013 --> 00:10:44,524
Nowhere.

301
00:10:44,524 --> 00:10:47,271
The password lives only in the environment.

302
00:10:47,271 --> 00:10:50,336
That is why a pipeline can run this exact setup,

303
00:10:50,336 --> 00:10:52,763
with the password in its secret store,

304
00:10:52,763 --> 00:10:54,360
and nothing else changes.

305
00:10:54,910 --> 00:10:56,614
Look back at what we did.

306
00:10:56,614 --> 00:10:58,795
Two repositories with two rules.

307
00:10:58,795 --> 00:11:01,385
A role and a user that can do one job.

308
00:11:01,385 --> 00:11:03,839
Maven configured with matching I Ds,

309
00:11:03,839 --> 00:11:05,747
and no password in any file.

310
00:11:06,297 --> 00:11:07,972
We shipped a snapshot twice,

311
00:11:07,972 --> 00:11:09,108
and a release once.

312
00:11:09,108 --> 00:11:12,038
We watched Nexus refuse to overwrite the release.

313
00:11:12,038 --> 00:11:13,892
And we fetched everything back.

314
00:11:15,442 --> 00:11:16,893
Four mistakes to avoid.

315
00:11:17,443 --> 00:11:19,841
One: publishing with the admin account.

316
00:11:19,841 --> 00:11:21,256
If that password leaks,

317
00:11:21,256 --> 00:11:22,301
everything leaks.

318
00:11:22,301 --> 00:11:23,224
Use a deployer.

319
00:11:23,774 --> 00:11:25,481
Two: a password in the pom,

320
00:11:25,481 --> 00:11:28,453
or in a settings file that is committed to Git.

321
00:11:28,453 --> 00:11:30,097
Use environment variables,

322
00:11:30,097 --> 00:11:32,499
and the secret store of your pipeline.

323
00:11:33,049 --> 00:11:35,088
Three: I Ds that do not match.

324
00:11:35,088 --> 00:11:36,584
The pom says one name,

325
00:11:36,584 --> 00:11:38,351
the settings file another.

326
00:11:38,351 --> 00:11:40,186
Maven sends no credentials,

327
00:11:40,186 --> 00:11:41,817
and you get four oh one.

328
00:11:42,367 --> 00:11:46,129
Four: releasing into a repository that allows redeploy.

329
00:11:46,129 --> 00:11:49,344
Then one point oh point oh can silently change,

330
00:11:49,344 --> 00:11:50,986
and nobody can trust it.

331
00:11:52,536 --> 00:11:54,776
Your turn, on your own machine, in a lab.

332
00:11:55,326 --> 00:11:57,371
One: create two repositories,

333
00:11:57,371 --> 00:12:00,404
cloudcart-snapshots and cloudcart-releases.

334
00:12:00,404 --> 00:12:02,590
Snapshot with redeploy allowed,

335
00:12:02,590 --> 00:12:04,777
release with redeploy disabled.

336
00:12:05,327 --> 00:12:10,553
Two: create the role and the user. Check that the user has four privileges, and no more.

337
00:12:11,103 --> 00:12:16,223
Three: write the settings file with environment variables, and deploy a snapshot, twice.

338
00:12:16,773 --> 00:12:19,352
Four: release version one point oh point oh.

339
00:12:19,352 --> 00:12:20,993
Then try to deploy it again,

340
00:12:20,993 --> 00:12:22,107
and read the error.

341
00:12:22,657 --> 00:12:24,385
Pause the video, and do it now.

342
00:12:39,935 --> 00:12:40,810
Quick check.

343
00:12:41,360 --> 00:12:44,112
What is the difference between a snapshot and a release?

344
00:12:44,662 --> 00:12:47,072
Why does a deployer get its own role and user?

345
00:12:47,622 --> 00:12:50,844
Which two things must match between the pom and the settings file?

346
00:12:51,394 --> 00:12:54,316
Where does the password belong, and where must it never be?

347
00:12:54,866 --> 00:12:56,936
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

348
00:13:07,486 --> 00:13:10,970
Objectives covered: snapshot and release repositories,

349
00:13:10,970 --> 00:13:12,648
publishing and retrieving,

350
00:13:12,648 --> 00:13:15,294
and Maven settings with safe credentials.

351
00:13:15,844 --> 00:13:17,550
Next: making it trustworthy.

352
00:13:17,550 --> 00:13:18,829
Narrower permissions,

353
00:13:18,829 --> 00:13:19,743
clean-up rules,

354
00:13:19,743 --> 00:13:22,180
and proof of where every file came from.

355
00:13:22,730 --> 00:13:26,932
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss the next lesson.
